## Review: issue #52 — recovery-key generation and display screen

## Findings

### P1 — Leaving or restarting before acknowledgment permanently strands the recovery setup

`RecoveryKeyScreen` calls `setupRecoveryKey()` in `initState`
([recovery_key_screen.dart](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/screens/recovery_key_screen.dart):31), and that method immediately writes the recovery-key wrap and salt to `encryption_meta.json` before the user has copied, shared, or acknowledged the displayed plaintext key
([storage_service.dart](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/services/storage_service.dart):267). If the screen is disposed, the app is killed, or navigation returns to this step before the checkbox is accepted, a new screen instance calls `setupRecoveryKey()` again. Because the already-written `wrappedMasterKeyRecovery` is non-null, it throws `StateError` ([storage_service.dart](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/services/storage_service.dart):280); the UI reduces that to a generic error with no recovery action ([recovery_key_screen.dart](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/screens/recovery_key_screen.dart):58). The only plaintext key was held by the discarded widget, so it can never be displayed again even though the UI never allowed the user to confirm it was saved.

Do not persist the recovery wrapper merely to render the screen. Keep a candidate recovery key and its wrapped payload in the in-progress screen/flow, then atomically commit it only when the user acknowledges it and immediately continue. If the candidate is abandoned before that point, no recovery setup should exist and a later visit may safely create a new candidate. Alternatively, introduce an explicit durable pending/acknowledged state with a safe retry/rollback design; it must never leave a persisted wrap whose plaintext has been discarded. Add a regression widget/integration test that creates the screen, waits for a key, disposes or recreates it before acknowledgment, and verifies the user can still complete setup without silently replacing a committed key. The existing one-call-only test does not cover this transition.

`#53` owns the later password-or-recovery-key unlock UI and can add the actual recovery-unlock service entry point; this finding is about #52 committing irreversible recovery state before its own required acknowledgment gate.

